#{[17592186495053 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495053 #edb/ref 1071 "A $3$-pound bag of flour costs $\\[math] 6.42.$ What is the price per pound?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495053 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "05aac4e0-eee5-5923-80d0-059ceee6159d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495053 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the unit price, we divide the total cost by the number of pounds:\n\n$$\n6.42  \\div 3 = 2.14\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit price is $\\[math] 2.14$ per pound." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495054 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495054 #edb/ref 1069 "q-354909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495054 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495054 #edb/ref 1071 "At a gourmet shop, a $4$-cup container of artisanal soup costs $$11.20.$ What's the unit price per fluid ounce?\n\nAssume that there are $8$ fluid ounces in one cup." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495054 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "05c8cffe-2eeb-5afe-9742-13e5b3b2c630" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495054 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we convert from cups to fluid ounces. There are $8$ fluid ounces in each cup, so we multiply the number of cups by $8:$  \n\n$$\n4\\times 8=32\n$$\n\nSo, $4$ cups is the same as $32$ fluid ounces. Now, to find the unit price per fluid ounce, we divide the total price by $32:$  \n\n$$\n11.20÷32=0.35\n$$\n\nTherefore, the artisanal soup costs $$0.35$ per fluid ounce." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495061 #edb/ref 1069 "e-22088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495061 #edb/ref 1071 "A bottle contains $32$ fluid ounces of juice and costs $\\[math]8.00.$ What's the unit price per cup?\n\nAssume that there are $8$ fluid ounces in one cup." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495061 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13572493-e244-5164-b0c2-a6c101cfeecc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495061 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we convert from fluid ounces to cups. There are $8$ fluid ounces in each cup, so we divide the number of fluid ounces by $8\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n32 \\div 8 = 4\n$$\n\nSo, $32$ fluid ounces is the same as $4$ cups.  \n\nNow, to find the unit price per cup, we divide the total price by $4\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n8.00 \\div 4 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the juice costs $\\[math]2.00$ per cup." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495067 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495067 #edb/ref 1069 "q-354906" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495067 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495067 #edb/ref 1071 "Buying premium house paint costs $$156$ per gallon. What's the unit price per quart?\n\nAssume that there are $4$ quarts in one gallon." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495067 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f900baf-6fb2-5ced-9586-c12d0492133f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495067 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that there are $4$ quarts in $1$ gallon. So, to find the unit price per quart, we divide the total price by $4:$\n\n$$\n156÷4=39\n$$\n\nTherefore, buying the paint costs $$39$ per quart." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495080 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495080 #edb/ref 1069 "q-354921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495080 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495080 #edb/ref 1071 "David bought $1$ foot of decorative ribbon and paid $$1.20$ for it. What's the unit price per yard?\n\nAssume that there are $3$ feet in one yard." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495080 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "439aee16-5dee-54f4-90f1-94c1e1c52d92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495080 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that there are $3$ feet in each yard.  \n\nSince the price is $$1.20$ per foot, to find the price per yard, we multiply by $3:$\n\n$$\n1.20\\times 3=3.60\n$$\n\nTherefore, the decorative ribbon costs $$3.60$ per yard." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495087 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495087 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68490" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495087 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495087 #edb/ref 1071 "A bag of $50$ medium sized apples costs $$25.$ What's the unit price per apple?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495087 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "577e3bd3-7831-5697-9d2d-c928a9f97262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495087 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the unit price per apple, we divide the total cost by the number of apples:\n\n$$\n25÷50=0.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit price is $$0.50$ per apple." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495090 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495090 #edb/ref 1069 "q-354910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495090 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495090 #edb/ref 1071 "At a craft store, a $12$-yard roll of copper wire costs $$19.80.$ What's the unit price per foot?\n\nAssume that there are $3$ feet in one yard." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495090 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5fae9bf2-7c1b-5d11-aed5-585d2966343f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495090 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we convert from yards to feet. There are $3$ feet in each yard, so we multiply the number of yards by $3:$  \n\n$$\n12\\times 3=36\n$$\n\nSo, $12$ yards is the same as $36$ feet. Now, to find the unit price per foot, we divide the total price by $36:$  \n\n$$\n19.80÷36=0.55\n$$\n\nTherefore, the copper wire costs $$0.55$ per foot." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495101 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495101 #edb/ref 1069 "q-354879" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495101 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495101 #edb/ref 1071 "A bag of five apples costs $$3.75.$ What is the unit price per apple?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495101 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7afa9cdc-11d7-5a67-8844-54f32a535758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495101 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the unit price, we divide the total cost by the number of apples:\n\n$$\n3.75÷5=0.75\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit price is $$0.75$ per apple." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495104 #edb/ref 1069 "q-354927" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495104 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495104 #edb/ref 1071 "A chef bought $80$ ounces of spices and paid $$45.60$ for them. What's the unit price per pound?\n\nAssume that there are $16$ ounces in one pound." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495104 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80687806-40a5-5de4-a9f6-85c3a9e00b61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495104 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we convert from ounces to pounds. There are $16$ ounces in each pound, so we divide the number of ounces by $16:$     \n\n$$\n80÷16=5\n$$\n\nSo, $80$ ounces is the same as $5$ pounds. Now, to find the unit price per pound, we divide the total price by $5:$ \n\n$$\n45.60÷5=9.12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spices cost $$9.12$ per pound." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495113 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495113 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495113 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495113 #edb/ref 1071 "Repaving a country road costs $$711$  per yard. What's the unit price per foot?\n\nAssume that there are $3$ feet in one yard." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495113 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9154bcfc-535d-5b52-8c6a-5ddc0039e572" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495113 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that there are $3$ feet in $1$ yard. So, to find the unit price per foot, we divide the total price by $3:$\n\n$$\n711÷3=237\n$$\n\nTherefore, resurfacing the road costs $$237$ per foot." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495115 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495115 #edb/ref 1069 "q-354880" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495115 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495115 #edb/ref 1071 "A pack of four batteries costs $$5.40.$ What is the unit price per battery?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495115 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9377e6e7-cfb3-5dc7-8bbc-468ea9c5dc92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495115 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the unit price, we divide the total cost by the number of batteries:\n\n$$\n5.40÷4=1.35\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit price is $$1.35$ per battery." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495123 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495123 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495123 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495123 #edb/ref 1071 "Thomas filled a $20$-quart container with gasoline and paid $$16.80$ for it.  What's the unit price per gallon?  \n\nAssume that there are $4$ quarts in one gallon." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495123 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a7e5dcb2-f757-5065-9783-df93e6eff2aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495123 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we convert from quarts to gallons. There are $4$ quarts in each gallon, so we divide the number of quarts by $4:$     \n\n$$\n20÷4=5\n$$\n\nSo, $20$ quarts is the same as $5$ gallons. Now, to find the unit price per gallon, we divide the total price by $5:$ \n\n$$\n16.80÷5=3.36\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gasoline costs $$3.36$ per gallon." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495124 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495124 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495124 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495124 #edb/ref 1071 "At a supermarket, a  box containing $80$ tea bags costs $$2.40.$ What is the unit price per bag?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495124 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a85453cd-6501-5fc6-8d99-8654b8a22521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495124 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the unit price, we divide the total cost by the number of bags:\n\n$$\n2.40÷80=0.03\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit price is $$0.03$ per bag." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495127 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495127 #edb/ref 1069 "q-354932" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495127 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495127 #edb/ref 1071 "Sarah bought $1$ ounce of scented soap and paid $$1.25$ for it. What's the unit price per pound?\n\nAssume that there are $16$ ounces in one pound." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495127 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af7b9054-324a-5b9b-bc1b-f5ed085967ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495127 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that there are $16$ ounces in each pound.  \n\nSince the price is $$1.25$ per ounce, to find the price per pound, we multiply by $16:$\n\n$$\n1.25\\times 16=20\n$$\n\nTherefore, the scented soap costs $$20$ per pound." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495130 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495130 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495130 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495130 #edb/ref 1071 "A pack contains $24$ fluid ounce of cream cheese and costs $$6.00.$ What's the unit price per cup?\n\nAssume that there are $8$ fluid ounces in one cup." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495130 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7148b05-c37b-56db-9e21-9c51d5a86ad3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495130 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we convert from fluid ounces to cups. There are $8$ fluid ounces in each cup, so we divide the number of fluid ounces by $8:$  \n\n$$\n24÷8=3\n$$\n\nSo, $24$ fluid ounces is the same as $3$ cups. Now, to find the unit price per cup, we divide the total price by $3:$ \n\n$$\n6.00÷3=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cream cheese costs $$2.00$ per cup." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495133 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495133 #edb/ref 1069 "q-354902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495133 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495133 #edb/ref 1071 "Installing chain-link fencing costs $$12.75$ per yard. What's the unit price per foot?\n\nAssume that there are $3$ feet in one yard." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495133 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b84fb10f-3175-5407-8a39-607c3eb8079e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495133 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that there are $3$ feet in $1$ yard. So, to find the unit price per foot, we divide the total price by $3:$\n\n$$\n12.75÷3=4.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, installing the fencing costs $$4.25$ per foot." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495163 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495163 #edb/ref 1069 "q-354980" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495163 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495163 #edb/ref 1071 "A container holding $1.5$ liters of organic apple juice costs $$6.75.$ What is the unit price per liter?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495163 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec846e5f-c89d-5f7d-9037-5f6b55231c56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495163 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the unit price, we divide the total cost by the number of liters:\n\n$$\n6.75÷1.5=4.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit price is $$4.50$ per liter." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495168 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495168 #edb/ref 1071 "A $5$-gallon jug of water costs $\\[math] 7.60.$ What is the price per quart?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495168 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f60f0711-fce9-525f-a2d1-fdf86ccef7b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495168 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we convert from gallons to quarts. There are $4$ quarts in each gallon, so we multiply the number of gallons by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n5 \\times 4 = 20\n$$\n\nSo $5$ gallons is the same as $20$ quarts. Now, to find the price per quart, we divide the total price by $20\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n7.60 \\div 20 = 0.38\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water costs $\\[math] 0.38$ per quart." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495184 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495184 #edb/ref 1061 616165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495184 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1885e554-32d8-511b-808d-67d60f7399df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495184 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495214 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495214 #edb/ref 1061 616169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495214 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "76ea330a-6861-5c40-a2ba-13e287e50b81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495214 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495239 #edb/ref 1061 616172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495239 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aedb1d7d-de1a-5c5e-bb8b-a9a5130bbd17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495239 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495248 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495248 #edb/ref 1061 616164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495248 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd03cc48-369a-5495-aa96-7aa335bd67c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495248 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495249 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495249 #edb/ref 1061 616168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495249 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1f9c4d4-8e93-5221-9ae9-b9e48f28f0d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495249 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495276 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186495041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495276 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186495044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495276 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186495045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495276 #edb/ref 1031 "Finding Unit Prices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495276 #edb/ref 1033 2555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495276 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "cef7c109-a883-5959-842c-c3cb8202ae04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495277 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose that installing a fence costs $\\[math]360$ per yard. What would be the unit price per foot?\n\nFirst, notice that there are $3$ feet in $1$ yard.  \n\nTo find the unit price per foot, we *divide* the price per yard by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n360 \\div 3 = 120\n$$\n\nTherefore, installing the fence costs $\\[/math]120$ per foot.\n\nIn reverse, if we know that installing a fence costs $\\[math]120$ per foot, to find the unit price per yard, we would need to *multiply* the price per foot by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n120 \\times 3 = 360\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495277 #edb/ref 1065 15094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495277 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "15c0e757-69bb-5bfd-a417-6de7c79fa333" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495277 #edb/ref 1067 "Unit Prices With Units Conversions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495279 #edb/ref 1064 "A **unit price** is the price of a single item. To compute a unit price, we divide the total cost of a group of items by the number of items in the group.\n\nFor example, suppose a store sells a pack of $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12.$ \n\nTo find the unit price, we divide the total cost by the number of shirts:\n\n$$\n12 \\div 3  = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit price is $\\[/math]4$ per shirt." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495279 #edb/ref 1065 3104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495279 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1e8ab0cc-4a5e-57ca-a168-f89d110ff113" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495279 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
